The Nautilus DevOps team is planning to test several Ansible playbooks on different app servers in Stratos DC. Before that, some pre-requisites must be met. Essentially, the team needs to set up a password-less SSH connection between Ansible controller and Ansible managed nodes. One of the tickets is assigned to you; please complete the task as per details mentioned below:
a. Jump host is our Ansible controller, and we are going to run Ansible playbooks through thor user from jump host.
b. There is an inventory file /home/thor/ansible/inventory on jump host. Using that inventory file test Ansible ping from jump host to App Server 2, make sure ping works

Solution
Yes. The goal is to make sure thor on the Jump host can SSH to App Server 2 without a password, then verify it through the existing inventory.

Since you’re already on the Jump host, do this in order:

1. Check the existing inventory
cat /home/thor/ansible/inventory

You should see an entry for stapp02 (App Server 2), likely with connection details such as:

[app]
stapp02 ansible_host=stapp02 ansible_user=<user>
2. Check whether thor already has an SSH key
ls -la /home/thor/.ssh/

If you don't have a private key such as id_rsa, create one:

ssh-keygen -t rsa -N "" -f /home/thor/.ssh/id_rsa

Just press Enter if prompted for anything else.

3. Copy the public key to App Server 2

First determine the SSH user from the inventory:

grep -n "stapp02" /home/thor/ansible/inventory

Then use:

ssh-copy-id <user>@stapp02

For example, if the inventory says the user is tony:

ssh-copy-id tony@stapp02

Enter the App Server 2 password when prompted. This is only needed once.

4. Test password-less SSH
ssh <user>@stapp02

If it logs in without asking for a password, exit:

exit
5. Run the required Ansible ping

Use the inventory exactly as the ticket specifies:

cd /home/thor/ansible
ansible -i inventory stapp02 -m ping

A successful result should look approximately like:

stapp02 | SUCCESS => {
    "changed": false,
    "ping": "pong"
}
